## What happened

Step 0 confirmed the bug, so I proceeded. Two independent verifications before touching anything: a standalone rustc transcription of glob_match returned true for git * main vs git -c core.sshCommand=curl_evil.sh push main, and the test I wrote first failed at exactly that row (`left: Some(true), right: None). A pattern a user writes meaning "the *` is the subcommand slot" was auto-approving an arbitrary command for the whole session.

The fix. glob_match untouched — two consumers depend on it ([permissions], and [directories] since #823). Instead a pure allow_wildcard_swallows_options, consulted only on the allow branch:

• fires only for a non-trailing * → cargo *, git commit -m * byte-identical to before (the regression risk, and essentially every existing user)
• when it fires, rejects an option token not written verbatim in the pattern → git * --force still approves git push --force
• rejection = "not auto-approved", never "refused" — falls through to the normal prompt, so no new warning was needed; the doc comment says so explicitly so a later reader doesn't add one

`deny` keeps plain `glob_match`, and the asymmetry is written into the code rather than only the docs: narrowing an allow removes privilege; narrowing a deny makes a fence stop matching — it fails open.

Verification. Six rows at the emission point (the Option<bool> a caller receives), four of them near-miss guards. Positive control run: neutering the helper fails the hole row, restoring it returns green.

Two judgment calls worth naming:

• Paid off my own register drift (config.rs 3388 → 3486). At +98 it was only a warning, but 98-of-100 means the next task in this file trips fatal for an unrelated reason — and Day 174 already cost me eleven absorbed entries for exactly this.
• Left `help.rs`'s +41 alone. Not my drift, never opened the file. Laundering someone else's into my commit is the opposite of what the ratchet is for.

The limit, stated rather than implied: this narrows allow matching only. It is not a claim that permissions.allow is now safe against every option-injection shape — it closes one class.
